---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Support from "../components/Support.astro";
import { DOWNLOAD_APK, DOWNLOAD_COMPANION, DOWNLOAD_SUMS, RELEASES, VIGEM } from "../consts";
---

<Base
  title="Set up your phone as a Windows controller"
  description="Install the PhonePad companion on Windows and the app on Android, pair once, and play. About five minutes, over Wi-Fi or your phone's own hotspot."
>
  <section>
    <div class="wrap prose">
      <h1 style="font-size:clamp(2rem,5vw,3rem)">Getting PhonePad working</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        About five minutes, most of it a one-time driver install. You need a
        Windows PC and an Android phone on the same Wi-Fi network, or the PC
        connected to the phone's own hotspot or USB tethering.
      </p>

      <h2>1. Install the ViGEmBus driver</h2>
      <p>
        Windows will not let a normal program invent a controller out of
        nothing. ViGEmBus is the driver that makes it possible — the same one
        Parsec and DS4Windows use. You install it once and never think about it
        again.
      </p>
      <p>
        <a class="btn btn-ghost" href={VIGEM} rel="noopener">
          Download ViGEmBus 1.22.0
        </a>
      </p>
      <p class="small muted">
        Grab <code>ViGEmBus_1.22.0_x64_x86_arm64.exe</code> from that page and
        run it. A reboot is not usually needed.
      </p>

      <h2>2. Run the PhonePad companion</h2>
      <p>
        Download <code>PhonePad-Companion.exe</code> and run it. There is no
        installer: it is one self-contained program, and deleting it uninstalls
        it.
      </p>
      <p>
        <a class="btn btn-primary" href={DOWNLOAD_COMPANION} rel="noopener">
          Download the Windows companion
        </a>
      </p>
      <p class="small muted">
        Windows SmartScreen may warn about an unrecognised publisher the first
        time; the companion is not code-signed. Checksums for every file are in
        <a href={DOWNLOAD_SUMS} rel="noopener">SHA256SUMS.txt</a> on the
        <a href={RELEASES} rel="noopener">release page</a>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Windows will ask whether to allow it through the firewall. Say
        <strong>yes for private networks</strong> — PhonePad only ever talks to
        your own network, and blocking it means your phone can never find your
        PC. Whatever you answered, if Windows is blocking PhonePad on a network
        this PC is on, the companion shows it under
        <em>Windows is blocking PhonePad</em> with the fix.
      </p>
      <p>
        You should now see <strong>Waiting for your phone</strong> and a
        six-digit pairing code.
      </p>

      <h2>3. Install the app</h2>
      <p>
        Download <code>PhonePad-arm64.apk</code> onto the phone and open it.
        Android will warn you about installing from an unknown source and ask
        you to allow it for your browser or file manager. That warning is what
        Android says about any app not distributed through the Play Store;
        PhonePad is not on the Play Store.
      </p>
      <p>
        <a class="btn btn-primary" href={DOWNLOAD_APK} rel="noopener">
          Download the Android app
        </a>
      </p>

      <h2>4. Pair, once</h2>
      <p>
        Open PhonePad. Your PC appears within a second or two. Tap it, type the
        six digits shown on the PC, and you are on the controller.
      </p>
      <p>
        That code is used once, to establish a key the two devices keep. From
        then on, opening the app and tapping your PC goes straight through — no
        code, no IP addresses, nothing to remember. If you mistype it, the PC
        shows a new code: each one is good for a single attempt.
      </p>
      <p class="small muted">
        Phone and PC both need PhonePad 1.1 or newer to pair. A phone that was
        already paired with 1.0 keeps working.
      </p>

      <h2>No Wi-Fi router? Use the phone's hotspot</h2>
      <p>
        Turn on the phone's <strong>hotspot</strong> and connect the PC to it,
        or plug the phone in and turn on <strong>USB tethering</strong>. Then
        open PhonePad as usual; it looks on every network the phone is part of.
      </p>
      <p>
        The first time, Windows will be blocking it: it files every network it
        has not seen before as <em>public</em>. The companion shows that network
        under <em>Windows is blocking PhonePad</em> with a
        <strong>Make private</strong> button. That marks just that network as
        one you trust, with one administrator prompt, and Windows remembers it
        for a hotspot. With USB tethering, many phones look like a new network
        each time tethering starts, so you may be asked again.
      </p>
      <p class="small muted">
        Only do this for your own phone or your own network, never for café,
        hotel or airport Wi-Fi. PhonePad itself never opens its ports on public
        networks.
      </p>

      <h2>5. Check Windows agrees</h2>
      <p>
        Press <kbd>Win</kbd>+<kbd>R</kbd>, type <code>joy.cpl</code>, press
        Enter. You should see <em>Xbox 360 Controller for Windows</em>. Open its
        properties and the buttons and axes should light up as you touch the
        phone.
      </p>
      <p>
        If that works, every XInput game on the machine will see the pad. There
        is nothing to configure per game.
      </p>

      <hr />

      <h2>Playing cloud games</h2>
      <p>
        Xbox Cloud Gaming works, with one thing to know:
        <strong>launch the game first, then hold the XBOX button on the phone</strong>.
        There is a real reason for it, explained in
        <a href="/xbox-cloud-gaming-phone-controller/">the cloud gaming guide</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>If something is wrong</h2>
      <p>
        The <a href="/troubleshooting/">troubleshooting page</a> covers what
        actually goes wrong, in roughly the order it goes wrong.
      </p>

      <div style="margin-top:3rem">
        <Support />
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
